Module 3, branch the flow of code using the switch case construct in C-sharp.

01:11:04.080 --> 01:11:06.080
And we're on Unit 2 of this module.

01:11:06.080 --> 01:11:15.080
A switch statement is a C-sharp selection statement that provides an alternative to an if-else if-else branching construct.

01:11:15.080 --> 01:11:26.080
The switch statement provides advantages over an if-else if-else construct when evaluating a single value against a list of known matching values.

01:11:26.080 --> 01:11:33.080
Consider the following scenario. You're working on an application related to food nutrition.

01:11:33.080 --> 01:11:44.080
A section of the code deals with fruits. Your code includes a variable named fruit that's used to hold the name of the different types of fruit.

01:11:44.080 --> 01:11:49.080
You have a list of the 20 fruits that your application is focused on.

01:11:49.080 --> 01:11:54.080
You want to branch your code based on the value assigned to fruit.

01:11:54.080 --> 01:12:00.080
In this scenario, you can use a switch statement to create a separate branch for each type of fruit.

01:12:00.080 --> 01:12:07.080
Further down the page, we are guided through a coding exercise to help us understand switch statements.

01:12:07.080 --> 01:12:12.080
Let's go through the step-by-step instructions for this coding exercise.

01:12:12.080 --> 01:12:18.080
Ensure that you have Visual Studio Code open and Program.cs displayed in the Editor panel.

01:12:18.080 --> 01:12:41.080
Type the following code into the Visual Studio Code Editor. So let's do that.

01:12:41.080 --> 01:12:47.080
Basically, in this switch statement, you are evaluating the employee-level variables value.

01:12:47.080 --> 01:12:54.080
In the case where the employee-level variable equals 100, the title variable is set to junior associate.

01:12:54.080 --> 01:13:04.080
In the case where the employee-level variable value is 200, the title variable is set to a string value of senior associate.

01:13:04.080 --> 01:13:11.080
In the case where the employee-level variable is set to 300, the title variable is set to a value of manager.

01:13:11.080 --> 01:13:16.080
If the employee level variable value does not match any of these cases,

01:13:16.080 --> 01:13:22.080
the code within the default section is run and the title variable is set to Associate.

01:13:22.080 --> 01:13:26.080
On the Visual Studio Code file menu, select Save.

01:13:26.080 --> 01:13:33.080
The program.cs file must be saved before building or running the code.

01:13:33.080 --> 01:13:39.080
At the terminal command prompt, type.NET run and then press Enter.

01:13:39.080 --> 01:13:43.080
Note, if you see a message saying, couldn't find a project to run,

01:13:43.080 --> 01:13:50.080
ensure that the terminal command prompt displays the expected test project folder location.

01:13:50.080 --> 01:13:59.080
You should see the following output.

01:13:59.080 --> 01:14:03.080
Take a minute to review the switch statement that you entered.

01:14:03.080 --> 01:14:09.080
Notice that the switch statement defines a single code block that includes a list of switch sections.

01:14:09.080 --> 01:14:16.080
To the right of the switch keyword is a switch expression that's enclosed in parentheses.

01:14:16.080 --> 01:14:20.080
Notice the list of switch sections inside the code block.

01:14:20.080 --> 01:14:23.080
The switch code block contains a list of switch sections,

01:14:23.080 --> 01:14:26.080
each of which includes one or more switch labels.

01:14:26.080 --> 01:14:30.080
In addition, each switch section includes a statement list

01:14:30.080 --> 01:14:37.080
that will execute if the label is equal to the switch expression defined at the top of the switch statement.

01:14:37.080 --> 01:14:42.080
The switch expression is evaluated against the case labels from top to bottom

01:14:42.080 --> 01:14:46.080
until a value that is equal to the switch expression is found.

01:14:46.080 --> 01:14:53.080
If none of the labels are a match, the statement list for the default case will be executed.

01:14:53.080 --> 01:14:59.080
If no default is included, control is transferred to the endpoint of the switch statement.

01:14:59.080 --> 01:15:07.080
Each label must provide a value type that matches the type specified in the switch expression.

01:15:07.080 --> 01:15:13.080
Note the optional default label can appear at any position within the list of switch sections.

01:15:13.080 --> 01:15:17.080
However, most developers choose to put it last

01:15:17.080 --> 01:15:23.080
because it makes more sense logically to position default as the final option.

01:15:23.080 --> 01:15:27.080
Regardless of the position, the default section will be evaluated last.

01:15:27.080 --> 01:15:32.080
In our example, the switch expression is employee level.

01:15:32.080 --> 01:15:37.080
Each switch section has a single switch label, case or default.

01:15:37.080 --> 01:15:45.080
The matching switch section is defined by case 200 since employee level equals 200.

01:15:45.080 --> 01:15:49.080
Notice that each switch section is separated from the next.

01:15:49.080 --> 01:15:52.080
Only one switch section is allowed to be executed.

01:15:52.080 --> 01:15:59.080
This means the execution of a switch section is not permitted to fall through to the next switch section.

01:15:59.080 --> 01:16:07.080
The break keyword is one of several ways to end a switch section before it gets to the next section.

01:16:07.080 --> 01:16:15.080
If you forget the break keyword or optionally the return keyword, the compiler will generate an error.

01:16:15.080 --> 01:16:20.080
Change the level variable value to see how the switch statement evaluates it.

01:16:20.080 --> 01:16:27.080
To exercise the default case, let's change the employee's level by modifying the value assignment.

01:16:27.080 --> 01:16:40.080
So if we assign the value 201 to the employee level variable and we run the code,

01:16:40.080 --> 01:16:45.080
